  <h2>Revenue Model</h2>
  <p>
    The Retreat Center is one of BARK's highest-potential revenue sources. Conservative projections
    based on the comparable market:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guest pricing</strong> — $1,000&ndash;$2,000 per week per guest, depending on season and program tier</li>
    <li><strong>Capacity</strong> — 8&ndash;12 guests per retreat (intimate group size is a feature, not a constraint)</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency</strong> — 20&ndash;30 retreats per year, with breaks for community use and maintenance</li>
    <li><strong>Revenue range</strong> — $160,000&ndash;$720,000 per year at full operation</li>
    <li><strong>Operating costs</strong> — Estimated 40&ndash;55% of revenue (food, facilitator compensation, equipment maintenance, marketing). Bolivia's cost structure is the multiplier.</li>
    <li><strong>Net contribution</strong> — $70,000&ndash;$400,000 per year returned to the cooperative</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These figures assume gradual ramp-up. Year 1 is likely 8&ndash;12 retreats with 6&ndash;8 guests average.
    Full capacity is a Year 3+ target. Revenue is reinvested per cooperative statute — the Retreat Center
    funds community infrastructure, not private profit.
  </p>

  <h2>How Retreats Build Community</h2>
  <p>
    The retreat center is not just a revenue engine. It is how the community grows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every retreat guest arrives with skills, capital, networks, and curiosity. Some return. Some invest.
    Some relocate. The retreat program is designed to give guests a genuine experience of the community —
    not tourism, but temporary participation. Guests work alongside residents, learn the systems, contribute
    their expertise. The ones who stay change the community's DNA.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is deliberate. A week living in the community — eating food from the gardens, meeting the builders,
    understanding the governance, breathing the air — is more persuasive than any pitch deck. The retreat
    is BARK's most effective recruiting tool, its most honest introduction, and its most natural filter.
    People who thrive here know it within days. People who don't, leave with a good experience and no hard feelings.
  </p>

  <h2>From Retreat to Longevity District</h2>
  <p>
    The Retreat Center is the seed of something larger. The same infrastructure that monitors meditation
    brainwaves can monitor aging biomarkers. The same community that supports healing retreats can support
    long-term longevity programs. The evolution is natural:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Phase 1: Technoshamanic tools</strong> — Biometric monitoring, EEG, HRV, plant medicine research. This is where we start: quantified wellness retreats that generate real physiological data and build the community's capacity for health technology.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 2: Medical tools</strong> — Develop diagnostic and therapeutic devices for retreat guests. On-site blood panels, metabolic testing, sleep analysis. The Techno Park builds the instruments; the retreat center deploys them. Guests leave with a health baseline they can track over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 3: Anti-aging lab</strong> — Partner with longevity researchers. Offer extended programs for aging intervention: NAD+ protocols, senolytics, epigenetic testing (biological age vs. chronological age), personalized longevity protocols. Guests come for 2–4 week programs focused specifically on measurable health extension.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 4: Longevity district</strong> — A quiet, safe place in Bolivia, far from wars, stress, and force projection. Housing for long-term residents focused on longevity. Clean air at 1,750m, year-round mild climate, low stress, organic food from on-site farms, community support, and access to the anti-aging lab's ongoing research and protocols.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The pitch writes itself: <em>Where else can you live at 1,750m in a cloud forest, eat food grown 50 meters
    from your table, breathe air that hasn't passed through a city, and have access to cutting-edge longevity
    research — for a fraction of what it costs in California or Switzerland?</em>
  </p>

  <h2>What Happens Here</h2>
  <ul>
